  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite and RAP analysis reveals a vigorous \nupper level shortwave in the vicinity of Lake Winnipeg diving \nsoutheast towards the northern Great Lakes. At the surface, a \ndeepening low pressure stacked below the parent wave will continue \nnorth of Lake Superior today. Mid to upper level ascent on the nose \nof a 100kt 300mb jet streak will be the forcing mechanism for a \nfirst round of potentially strong to severe storms this morning into \nthe early afternoon. As of ~2:30 am edt, convection has been ongoing \nin upstream Ontario and a few thunderstorms are working across \neastern Superior. CAM guidance suggests a line of showers and \nthunderstorms developing in MN and knocking on our western \nwaters/shorelines by ~12z this morning, however, there remains some \nuncertainty in the potential severity of this round given meager \ninstability forecasted then. At this time thinking a few strong \nstorms may be possible in the west half after daybreak given \nincrease strong synoptic ascent, but confidence remains low.\n\nThe main hazard is expected this afternoon into the evening as a \nfairly volatile atmosphere for our neck of the woods shapes up. As \nstrong synoptic support spreads over the area and airmass recover \ntakes place, CAM ensembles suggest MUCAPE values climbing near 2500 \nj/kg. What is most impressive is the kinematic environment. Strong \nmid-level flow will increase bulk shear between 45-55 kts and sickle \nshaped low-level hodographs support 0-1 km SRH upwards of 100-200 \nm2/s2 (150-250 1-3km SRH). As storm coverage increase ahead of and \nalong a surface cold front, all severe weather hazards are on the \ntable, including tornadoes should isolated supercells be maintained. \nSteep lapse rates aloft will contribute to a sizable hail threat in \nsupercells, potentially 2\" or greater. Once storms grow upscale into \na more linear segment, damaging winds will then become the main \nthreat. SPC has maintained a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for the \nentire UP with an area of the west and central UP in an Enhanced \nRisk (level 3 of 5) for severe weather. All of this is to say that \nif you have outdoor plans today, especially in the afternoon and \nevening, have a way to receive a warning and a plan should one be \nissued.\n\nStrengthening southerly winds on will result in building waves over \nLake Michigan, as high as 7 feet by late afternoon along the beaches \nof Schoolcraft County. This will bring a high rip current threat and \ndangerous swim conditions.\n\nExpect cooler conditions on Tuesday into Wednesday behind the cold \nfront that will sweep through the area tonight. High temperatures \nwill be mainly in the 70s with 60s possible close to Lake Superior. \nIt will be breezy on Tuesday with north winds potentially gusting to \n30 mph over the land areas and over 45 mph over the Great Lakes. \nSome lingering showers are likely for the NW wind \"snowbelts\" and \nupslope favored areas on Tuesday as 850 mb temps cool between 5-10C. \nThis may aid in lake-enhanced showers as the nearshores and areas of \nthe west and central lake have warmed between 15-20C. Swim \nconditions will likely become dangerous on Tuesday for the Lake \nSuperior beaches of Marquette and Alger County. Smoke could also \nincrease again with the northerly flow.\n\nThe area remains under northwest flow aloft into the early part of \nthe weekend with a ridge building back over the central U.S. This \nwill bring the opportunity for showers and thunderstorms as weak \ndisturbances in the flow push across the U.P. Afternoon temperatures \nwill be seasonable, upper 70s to low 80s, with lows in the 50s. \nTemperatures will warm to the mid 80s on Sunday as the ridge shifts \neast over the Upper Great Lakes.",
